038 #edb/ref 17592186317227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186063918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064318 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1045 "Defining Continuity at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064410 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4206463b-9991-5aa1-a8f1-1ddfceac3676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186403018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca98ec7d-0763-558c-b634-f1da93c05079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064888 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}({x}^{3}+x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1) \\\\  & =-2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065218 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065218 #edb/ref 1061 40541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065218 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "755b1ece-f8b7-54b2-8c09-f8c4ab79bfba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065219 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065219 #edb/ref 1061 40542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065219 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6c61bd4-670b-5aba-b3d3-f57de24f1e4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065219 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065220 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065220 #edb/ref 1061 40545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065220 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53f7255d-8a19-5fff-954a-4e51d40ed568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065220 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065221 #edb/ref 1061 40548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065221 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f203cd2d-bb6f-5e30-824f-98ee8ab90684" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065221 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065222 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065222 #edb/ref 1061 40849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065222 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7d6d175-7180-513d-95c8-c96d9df56bce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065222 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065401 #edb/ref 1064 "Intuitively, a function $f(x)$ is **continuous** if we can draw $y=f(x)$ without taking our pen off the paper. \n\nWe can give a precise mathematical definition of continuity using limits:\n\n> *A function $f(x)$ is **continuous at the point** $\\boldsymbol{x=c}$ if*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c).\n> $$\n\nIn other words, a function is continuous at a point if the limit at the point is equal to the function value at the point.\n\nTo test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist? In other words, we must check that $f(x)$ is defined at $x=c.$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist? In other words, we have to check that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to c^{-}}f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to c^{+}}f(x).$\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nIf the answer to all three questions is yes, then the function is continuous at $x=c.$ If the function is not continuous at $x=c,$ we say that it has a **discontinuity** at $x=c.$\n\nThe diagram below shows a function that's continuous at $x=c.$\n\n![](images/54/54ebdb8faa4b1ece5620b817b813780e8abd5cc89cf858eed4cb9ba03f24a046.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(c)$ exists$\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exists $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)$ $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nLet's now see how to use these continuity conditions to check for continuity in a piecewise function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065401 #edb/ref 1065 6695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065401 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4a7b79ea-d89f-5d21-948b-8a735e641995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065401 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065402 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that to test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to c}f(x) = f(c)?$\n\nLet's use these three conditions to show that the following function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3-x & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe check all three conditions in turn.\n\n- Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)= 1+1= 2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3-x) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 1^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists: \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 \\:\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- We have that $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 1} f(x) = 2 = f(1).$ $\\:\\: {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c55aa89d279d94b7561f6c24ebdb37bafcf46dbc20edf4f93d515aca7ae4cde6.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065402 #edb/ref 1065 6746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065402 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ceec35c-4700-5ccf-a376-7a6c1fcd5e94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065402 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186067179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067174 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5ce676b-13f0-56a5-846b-a845f49e5220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067175 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8ffa3959-bcae-53d4-b750-3acd046f4817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067176 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6f13b0a-3767-5aac-9371-497ed9cc05f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067177 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067177 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067177 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15c4250c-ec22-5c0f-9869-16bf0a21f119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067178 #edb/ref 1080 "Undefined because the curve and the line do not intersect." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067178 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067178 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "76e00e67-6b8c-5712-9e4a-778c86b82f69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067179 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067179 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186067179 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c894e31-8bab-5c7b-bdc4-511f737f9090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of Two Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068884 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4fafc8c8-0452-582f-b603-6f633de9a39a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186338788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection Points in the Case of One or No Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068885 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05669abb-838d-5b1c-b97f-833a28b1d413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Range of a Parameter Given Two, One, or No Intersection Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068886 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2180b368-597c-5b99-a9ef-346169711637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068965 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f7fe334b-af06-545b-8320-d04ce420b4ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1071 "If the line $y=-2x+3$ and the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1$ intersect at the point $(p,q),$ then $p+q=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "801b9317-386e-554f-9207-4d232469922d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069334 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1061 466045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b6e2fa7-06cd-5725-9235-7ae5c5dbd6a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1061 466046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "674f9cf9-a5de-5045-b6cb-e0e31e295966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1061 466050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "999d3467-9a05-5d20-9fd5-1b8783ff67e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1061 466051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "663d62ae-d9e6-5064-8a5b-bec6133beefb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1061 466054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c86492a6-57ef-5081-b122-627fc0b573d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069854 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1064 "When considering the intersection of a line and a reciprocal function, three possibilities can occur.\n\nThe first possibility is that there are two intersection points, as illustrated in the example below.\n\n![](images/00/00f19933ef5f2e7879012dbd9b87fd86964ed44b74e54749dccd2186e02e2a6a.png)\n\nThe second possibility is that there is only one intersection point. For example:\n\n![](images/aa/aabf0d5ea18c01842890b6e470d7c8eaaf36371565ff7c271ea2ab24ccfc9565.png)\n\nThe final possibility is that there are no intersection points. For example:\n\n![](images/da/da7ecba1887505f162144ab3b86e4cca9be104cda7e78ba8fef3d02f10de2abc.png)\n\nTypically, when computing the points of intersection of a reciprocal function and a line, we have to solve a system of two equations. In the first graph that was shown above, this system was\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system of equations can (usually) be transformed into a single rational equation, which can itself be converted into a quadratic equation. For the above system, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{x} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nBy solving the quadratic equation, we can find the intersection points. And like quadratic equations, there can be two, one, or no real solutions. In this case, there are two solutions:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\pm 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1065 3403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70a19ea7-8a66-5b98-b7a2-2312cfe9cf44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069975 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now turn our attention to the cases where a line and a reciprocal function intersect at either one point or no points.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find where the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}+1$ and the line $y=2$ intersect. To do this, we need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal and solving for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+1 & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the corresponding $y$-coordinate, we plug $x=1$ into either equation. Let's choose the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $\\left(1,\\,2 \\right)$.\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7da7ea3f7e2444197a7bc402309c22fe23d95483909d3d6674645101f036370.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1065 4596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68835670-e15c-543e-9f1c-ba5dfb7c1823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069983 #edb/ref 1067 "Intersections of Lines and Reciprocal Functions at One or No Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126386 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95824ee1-eaa5-5a70-8ab3-8de0d1f815bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 1s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126387 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d039f4a6-471f-5a6d-8c7b-7b6f20da9885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1080 "No solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126388 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6fbc6ac-7bd1-59a8-ba1b-7358d4ca8319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 3s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126389 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ed75ad4e-24ff-5a53-9276-d9d4a0a10469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 2s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "408ad7c7-e164-5b6a-884f-a6633e2eeb97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126391 #edb/ref 1080 "t = 6s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126391 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126391 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6cfe880c-c6a0-563e-aa89-65dcabd2f744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Speed of a Particle Given Its Velocity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129249 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0118bf72-c730-59f1-b722-85ed779f96c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129250 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c5146ef7-bd48-5d0d-903d-882d739b92c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Final Velocity of a Particle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129251 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7439a96f-07b1-5673-a103-af2c63c25d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186299730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Time when a Particle Reaches a Particular Velocity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186129072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129252 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "93d45138-b681-55c0-8910-1400bdf717c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1045 "Velocity and Acceleration for Plane Motion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186130176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129375 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b8fd4e1c-748f-577d-b086-ba515ee43c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186400737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(3i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-7i-6j)\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c82e497-5ec7-5624-8331-c5c43f9eb1a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129481 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130176 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130176 #edb/ref 1061 36897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130176 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f2baea5-8b66-5b0e-b2d7-72a64c3d40a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130176 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130177 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130177 #edb/ref 1061 36898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130177 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb3ae2a7-8c5a-5ef2-97b6-41dc1c9886bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130177 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130178 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130178 #edb/ref 1061 36900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130178 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d22baec-c09f-5f9f-b2ee-f15b2c144f57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130178 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130179 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130179 #edb/ref 1061 36901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130179 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f365715-3ac5-589f-a774-e29ae44744a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130179 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130180 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130180 #edb/ref 1061 36902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130180 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6eef6463-e817-5063-b678-359ac680a832" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130180 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130181 #edb/ref 1061 36906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130181 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2334ada5-75ed-5eef-a510-caabce5c0ae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130181 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130445 #edb/ref 1064 "We can describe the **velocity** of a particle moving in the $xy$-plane using a **velocity vector.** The velocity vector tells us the magnitude and direction at which the particle moves. We denote the velocity vector of a particle as $\\mathbf u.$\n\nAs an example, consider the particle $P$ shown below.\n\n![](images/88/8895fcf01aa16812144379c5ae004df694451d6e0a11a2b62e4dd62516f57316.png)\n\nIn this case, the velocity vector is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = (3\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\,\\mathbf{j}) \\: \\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nWe should think of the particle's velocity as being made up of two components: \n\n- a *horizontal* component of $3 \\: \\textrm{m/s}$ (moving to the right), and\n\n- a *vertical* component of $2 \\: \\textrm{m/s}$ (moving upward).\n\nIn other words, the particle moves $3$ meters to the right and $2$ units up every second. Also, since the horizontal and vertical components are constants, we say that the particle moves with **constant velocity.**\n\nThe magnitude of the velocity vector gives the **speed** of the particle. Therefore, in our example, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|u| & =|3i+2j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130445 #edb/ref 1065 6062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130445 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d6f4c5c9-7add-589a-a0ea-d52f3d02e40a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130445 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130446 #edb/ref 1064 "The acceleration of a particle tells us the rate at which its velocity changes over time. We can use vectors to model the acceleration of a particle moving in the $xy$-plane. \n\nFor example, consider a particle $P$ whose acceleration vector $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = (3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j})\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.\n$$\n\nWe should think of this particle's acceleration as being made up of two components: \n\n- a *horizontal* component of $3 \\: \\textrm{m/s}^2$ (to the right), and\n\n- a *vertical* component of $4 \\: \\textrm{m/s}^2$ (upward).\n\nIn other words,\n\n- the horizontal component of the velocity increases by $3\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ every second, and\n\n- the vertical component of the velocity increases by $4\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ every second.\n\nAlso, since both components are constants, we say that this particle moves with **constant acceleration**.\n\nIf a particle is moving with constant acceleration, the velocity vector $\\mathbf v$ at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{u}$ is the velocity of the particle when $t=0$ (i.e., the **initial velocity**).\n\nFor example, suppose that the initial velocity of our particle is given by $\\mathbf{u} = (2\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$ What is the particle's velocity when $t=2 \\, \\textrm{s}?$\n\nSubstituting our data into the formula above, we obtain that the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(2i+3j)+(3i+4j)(2) \\\\  & =(2i+3j)+(6i+8j) \\\\  & =(8i+11j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130446 #edb/ref 1065 6063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130446 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e71afcde-f4ec-52b0-bc4b-041b528e32f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130446 #edb/ref 1067 "Relating Velocity and Acceleration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186195380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186195381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186195382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186195383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186195384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195379 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69d9e92f-711c-5cfe-be85-f01c136b4027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195380 #edb/ref 1080 "images/0a/0a03f883f977e67b9299d52e1572e484ed6010dc4f2555c7cbc2048f193dbc7f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "957afc7c-bb56-510f-8194-063b82cf9c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195381 #edb/ref 1080 "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9d5bc49b-44d8-5eec-82fd-fbfe70cdcd9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195382 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ac/acccce074e5dee93bbca149c16c5f7794dce17d1cd16432d25b0132382d877f8.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186195382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b692d7e-4b16-534a-b4c7-20d13f0fff03" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
